    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "C&#8217;Mon In! An Island Kitchen Party"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "This summer, the Island’s best kitchen party is at The Mack. C&#8217;MON IN! throws open the door and invites you straight into a lively Maritime kitchen, where the party&#8217;s already started, the stories get bigger as the night goes on, and the music keeps everyone around the table just a little longer. Led by crowd favourites Robbie Doherty and Pierce Clarke, this laugh-out-loud, music-filled night brings together the songs Islanders know by heart and visitors can’t help but join in. With surprise moments, playful banter, and a band that knows how to keep the party rolling, you never quite know who’s going to walk through the kitchen door next. From foot-stomping classics to sing-along favourites, C’mon In! celebrates the very best of Atlantic Canadian music, including Stompin’ Tom, Anne Murray, Great Big Sea, and more. Grab a chair, sing along if you like, and settle in for one of the most fun nights out of the summer. &nbsp; &nbsp; Charlottetown Festival · Summer 2026 📅 2026 Festival Schedule ●Anne of Green Gables – The Musical™ ●Come From Away ●C&#8217;mon In! An Island Kitchen Party ●Polka Dot Door Complete calendar of performances at the Sobey Family Theatre, The Mack, and Outdoor [&hellip;]"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/keywords": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Comedy, Music, On Stage"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/eventStatus": [
      {
        "@id": "http://schema.org/EventScheduled"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/eventAttendanceMode": [
      {
        "@id": "http://schema.org/OfflineEventAttendanceMode"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/92b8dbe199a965ab",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/1.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Robbie Doherty"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Robbie Doherty is a Charlottetown-based entertainer and broadcaster known for his infectious energy and deep roots in Atlantic Canada’s music scene. He first gained national attention as the lead singer of the popular show band Sunday Punch, touring across Canada and earning multiple Music PEI award nominations.\r\n\r\nAfter stepping away from the band in 2014 to pursue professional curling – competing at an elite level, including four Tim Hortons Briers and a top-10 world ranking – Robbie returned to the stage with renewed creative focus. His children’s music project, Silly Robbie, produced two ECMA-nominated albums and a television series, Silly Robbie’s World, which aired across eight provinces.\r\n\r\nToday, Robbie brings his signature blend of humour, musicianship, and audience connection to C’Mon In! Atlantic Canada’s Best Kitchen Party, a hit live show now in its fourth season, which comes to The Mack in a new way. The production has become a favourite for its high-energy, feel-good celebration of East Coast music and culture.\r\n\r\nOffstage, Robbie is a seasoned sports broadcaster, working with Sportsnet and Curling Channel as a play-by-play announcer and reporter for the Grand Slam of Curling, along with coverage of hockey, basketball, and national multi-sport events – including the 2018 Winter Olympics.\r\n\r\nRobbie lives in Charlottetown with his wife, Jillian, and their two sons."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/584edb65df860652",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/2.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Pierce Clarke"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Pierce Clarke is a multi-instrumentalist and songwriter whose music blends emotion, genre, and storytelling into a sound that is both authentic and distinctly his own. Grounded in a love of craft and connection, his work reflects a belief that while every story is personal, music has the power to bring people together.\r\n\r\nOriginally from Prince Edward Island, Pierce studied songwriting at the Berklee College of Music, where he focused on both piano and voice. Since graduating, he has recorded and collaborated in studios across Toronto, New York, Boston, Miami, and Nashville, continually refining his sound and artistic voice.\r\n\r\nAs a performer, Pierce has toured nationally with Sunday Punch and led the Pierce Clarke Band on major stages, including the Cavendish Beach Music Festival. His latest creative venture, C’Mon In! Atlantic Canada’s Best Kitchen Party, co-created with longtime collaborator Robbie Doherty, brings his passion for live performance to the forefront, celebrating East Coast music with energy, humour, and heart.\r\n\r\nNow based once again on PEI, Pierce continues to evolve as a songwriter and performer, driven by a genuine love of music and a commitment to telling stories that resonate."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/067fbc24f761c094",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Putnam-Anne-Headshot-2023.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Anne Putnam"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "FOR THE CENTRE:\r\nAnne has Stage Managed many seasons for the Charlottetown Festival, primarily at The Mack for the past several years. Most recent include I’m Every Woman, The Songs of Johnny and June, and Becoming Dolly.\r\n\r\nFILM/TV:\r\nThe Adventures of the AfterMath Crew (Production Manager, APTN)\r\n\r\nTRAINING:\r\nBFA in Theatre Production\r\n\r\nAWARD:\r\nMaud Whitmore Scholarship\r\n\r\nAnne has worked in many theatres all across the country but is most happy working at home, here on The Island."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/fcdae71d36d300da",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Pat-Caron.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Pat Caron"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Pat Caron graduated from Holland College's School of Performing Arts in 2012 and began working at the Watermark Theatre shortly thereafter becoming the theatres Technical Director and Sound Designer in 2016.\r\n\r\nIn 2021 he founded Acting Badly Inc. which provides a wide range of services catered towards theatre and film.\r\n\r\nSome notable credits include Technical Director, Projection and Lighting Designer of Anne &amp; Gilbert The Musical (Florence Simmons Performance Hall), Sound Designer of The Play that Goes Wrong (Confederation Centre of Arts) and Lighting Director of Canada Games (Outdoor site, 2023)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/897e20a68d1444d9",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/3.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Luka Hall"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "FOR THE CENTRE:\r\nDebut\r\nPERFORMANCE:\r\nDynamic singer-songwriter, guitarist, and accomplished Prince Edward Island fiddle player known for blending traditional fiddle music with folk and country influences. Luka’s 2024 EP, To Be Honest, earned nominations for Traditional Roots Recording of the Year at the 2025 Music PEI Awards and Young Performer of the Year at the 2026 Canadian Folk Music Awards. In 2025, he released the original Christmas single Maybe Next Christmas with Brooke MacArthur.\r\nA compelling live performer, Luka has appeared at the PEI Festival of Small Halls, Rollo Bay Fiddle Festival, and Harmony House, toured with Irish Millie, and showcased at Ireland’s Your Roots Are Showing folk conference alongside Shane Pendergast. Luka is quickly gaining recognition as one of PEI’s rising young artists in the Canadian music scene.\r\nIG:\r\n\r\n@LukaHallMusic"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/5399c97a07225bca",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/2-1.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Cynthia MacLeod"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "FOR THE CENTRE:\r\nAtlantic Blue (2019), Spinning Yarns (2019)\r\nPERFORMANCE:\r\nOne of Prince Edward Island’s finest ambassadors of traditional music, Cynthia got her start performing on ceilidh stages across the Island as a young teenager before launching the acclaimed Brackley Beach Ceilidhs, which ran for ten successful seasons (2004–2013). Since 2003, she has represented PEI on tours across Canada and the United States, performed at multiple Canada Games opening and closing ceremonies (2010, 2022, 2023), the 2005 World Expo in Aichi, Japan, Celtfest Cuba (2010), Live with Regis &amp; Kelly (2010), aboard Norwegian Cruise Line (2013–2016), and on NHK Television in Japan (2025).\r\nA multi–Music PEI Award winner with five albums to her credit, Cynthia has received honours including Entertainer of the Year and Album of the Year. She regularly tours throughout New England and launched PEI Kitchen Parties in 2023, an entertainment company specializing in corporate and private events.\r\nIG:\r\n\r\n@macleodmusic"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/b2cc28495f890823",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/1-1.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Alan White"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "FOR THE CENTRE:\r\nThe Full Monty (Charlottetown Festival)\r\nPERFORMANCE:\r\n\r\nOriginally from Montague, Prince Edward Island, Alan grew up in a musical family where summer days sailing the Montague River often led to evening kitchen parties. After graduating with honours in music from Humber College in Toronto, Alan performed aboard cruise ships with a jazz quartet while sailing the Baltic, Mediterranean, and Caribbean seas. Since returning to PEI, he has been an instructor at Holland College&rsquo;s School of Performing Arts since 2011 while maintaining a diverse performance schedule. Recent credits include Against All Odds: A Night of Peter Gabriel and Phil Collins; Wouldn&rsquo;t It Be Nice: The Music of The Beach Boys; and These Dreams: The Music of Heart (Craig Fair Productions), along with performances with numerous local jazz, funk, soul, and rock bands.\r\n\r\nIG:\r\n\r\n@alan_white_drummer"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/97fd7ec4b2ac1aa7",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/JD.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Jeff Doherty"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "FOR THE CENTRE:\r\nDebut\r\nPERFORMANCE:\r\n\r\nMore than 15 years of live performance experience across Canada, appearing in venues ranging from intimate stages to large-scale productions. Jeff has collaborated with a wide range of respected East Coast artists, performed at numerous high-profile events and festivals, provided direct support for established country artists, and performed with many well-known tribute acts. Known for his versatility, professionalism, and consistency on stage, Jeff is grateful for the opportunity to share in the magic of live performance.\r\n\r\nIG:\r\n\r\n@jeffdoherty777"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/genid/48a9c66b06a49f95",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "https://confederationcentre.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Marli.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Marli Trecartin"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "FOR THE CENTRE:\r\nCharlottetown Festival debut\r\nPERFORMANCE:\r\nMarli (she/her) is known for her work throughout her birth province of New Brunswick and her chosen home of Prince Edward Island. Recent credits include Country Roads and Anything for Love: The Music of Meat Loaf (Craig Fair Productions). Other theatre credits include Provocation and What’s Eating You? (The Arts Guild); Bless You! and Realizations (Island Fringe Festival); Salt Water Moon (Kings Playhouse); as well as many seasons performing with Charlottetown Burlesque. A dedicated student of the arts, Marli will return to Holland College this fall to further her music education.\r\nIG:\r\n\r\n@marlitrecartin"
      }
    ]
  },
